# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md),
# CYCLE 1 on coolify - the first scenario authored in the php-laravel vertical.
#
# No cycles.jsonl exists for this repo (checked: verticals/php-laravel/results/loop/coolify/
# holds only author.agent.json and author.agent.log, and results/dryrun/coolify/ is empty),
# so this is a first draft and not a re-question. There is no trajectory to correct from and
# no side of the window to move away from; the next phase's two-arm run is the first
# measurement this scenario will ever face.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Honestly, and up front: the SET is an occurrence list, and this header says so rather
#   than dressing it up. `ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12` returned `0 candidate(s) above the
#   bar` with 0 rows / 0 files / 0 vias on all twelve anchors, so the holder kind is not on
#   the menu here (see the note at the end of this header about WHY that probe reads zero on
#   a PHP clone - it is not this phase's to fix).
#   What is NOT an occurrence list is what the ask demands PER element: the ROUTE by which
#   that place comes to be holding a server at all, and the one thing its own job would start
#   getting wrong. Almost nothing in the scored group looks a server up. It is handed one
#   already resolved as a promoted constructor property on a queued job, a listener or a
#   notification; or it walks to one across two or three relations on some OTHER model
#   (`$this->database->destination->server`, `$this->serviceDatabase->service->destination->server`);
#   or it reaches one through a string path (`data_get($this->database, 'destination.server')`)
#   that no symbol search resolves; or it comes back from an accessor on a third model that
#   intercepts lazy loading and hands out an identity-map cached instance. `grep -rn` prints
#   path:line; it does not print which of those four routes a line is on, it does not print
#   the enclosing routine, and it does not print the consequence. That is the citation cost
#   this cell charges.
#
# THE AXIS, IN ONE SENTENCE
#   Every scored dependent HOLDS a server it did not resolve - received as a promoted
#   constructor property, walked to across relations on another model, fetched through a
#   string relation path, or handed back by a caching accessor on a third model - and then
#   leans on one capability of it (remote execution, reachability, its settings record, its
#   address), so the dependency is a route and a capability rather than a mention.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, quoted, run in this session)
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Server --propose
#     app caller files : 45   (total incl test/non-app: 53)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 23
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   PRECISION 0.15 is the lowest of the three candidates profiled and it is close to the
#   0.078 that preceded a +1.00 cell, and far from the 0.78 of a deliberate control tie and
#   the ~0.94 of a dead cell. It RANKS; it decides nothing. The two other candidates profiled,
#   both quoted:
#     Application  35 app caller files / 18 dirs / 'Application' in 142 app files  PRECISION=0.25
#     PrivateKey   19 app caller files / 12 dirs / 'PrivateKey' in 42 app files    PRECISION=0.45
#   `Server` also tops anchor_rank.py --top 20 on this clone at 183 dependent files, ahead of
#   Team (75), ValidationPatterns (57) and Service (57), and it is the contract a coolify
#   maintainer would actually rework: every resource this product deploys is deployed onto one.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'
#   No min_confidence passed. Bare `symbol: Server` resolves cleanly to App\Models\Server (no
#   ambiguity flag), and the shown payload carries 46 direct_callers and 89 affected_via_composition
#   rows over 101 distinct files, `truncated: true`. All eighteen `dependents` rows and all four
#   anchors below appear in it by file. The payload's refs are the enclosing class or def; every
#   line pinned below was opened and read in this session, and no credit is taken from a tally.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py, quoted, run in this session)
#   {"ok": false, "ran": true, "recall": 0.589, "hits_n": 63, "truth_n": 107, "said_n": 118,
#    "samples": [0.589, 0.0, 0.0],
#    "reason": "closed-book recall 0.589 (63 members named) >= 0.3 - recited, both arms already know it"}
#   Read the payload, not the flag. What was recited is the CONTRACT CENTRE and nothing else:
#   the 20 recorded hits are `applications`, `booted`, `buildServers`, `checkSentinel`,
#   `databases`, `definedResources`, `destinations`, `dockerCleanupExecutions`, ...,
#   `isBuildServer`, `isCoolifyHost`, `isEmpty`, `isForceDisabled`, `isFunctional` - every one
#   of them a member of App\Models\Server itself, which is an ANCHOR here and does not score.
#   The intersection of the recited set with the eighteen scored rows' files, classes and
#   routines is EMPTY (checked in this session against `hits` and `answer_head`). Two of the
#   three samples came back 0.0. The standing law is that a famous repo is not disqualified and
#   a memorized ROW is; no row here is memorized, so no row was cut. What the recitation does
#   mean is that step 1's anchors are cheap for both arms, which is what anchors are for.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE BANKED-MASTODON SORT OF OCCURRENCE LIST AND NOT THE FIVE-FAILURE SORT
#   It is a two-step probe, so it cannot claim the session-pressure argument the banked cell
#   has (that ask sits at step 4 of 7). What it claims instead is measured only in the next
#   phase, and the honest statement of it is: nothing here has faced a baseline yet. The three
#   things this draft does have are the profile above (PRECISION 0.15, the lowest this bench
#   has profiled outside the +1.00 cell), a mechanism that is genuinely four different routes
#   rather than one, and a gold set curated on route rather than on token-darkness - sixteen of
#   the eighteen rows DO carry the token `Server` on or above the pinned line, which the
#   standing law says is fine, and one (`d:postgres-general`) does not carry it at all.
#   Whether that is enough is the mini-bench's call, not this file's.
#
# ONE DEFECT NAMED IN PASSING, NOT FIXED (scope lock)
#   `bench/lib/ring_sweep.py` reads the `retained_via_interfaces` payload and filters its rows
#   with `"_test.go" not in ref` (lines 120-123). That is a Go-shaped probe: on this PHP clone
#   it returns 0 rows for all twelve anchors, while the same clone's sense_blast payload carries
#   89 `affected_via_composition` rows for `Server` alone. So the empty ring here bounds the
#   Go retention signature and says nothing about whether PHP dependents hold this contract.
#   Named and left alone; the loop routes it, this phase does not.
# ============================================================================

name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the class that stands for one of the remote machines
  this product deploys onto - the thing that knows how to be reached, whether it is currently
  usable, and how a command gets run on it. The rework will change what that class hands back
  to the code that asks it questions, and it will change how a caller comes to be holding one
  in the first place. Before you touch it you have to know two things: how that class decides
  and reports that a machine is reachable and usable at all, and everywhere else in the
  codebase that would have to be reviewed or changed with it. Throughout, an answer that
  points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one
  phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set of names offered together
  with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not count, and a missed dependent
  is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start with the
      one part of this class's job that everything else leans on: deciding whether one of these
      machines can be worked with right now, and keeping that decision somewhere the rest of
      the system can read it. Trace that end to end. Show where the decision itself is computed
      and what it is composed of, where the state it reads is stored and kept up to date, where
      that state is refreshed by the work that goes out and checks the machine, and where a
      caller that has only an identifier gets back an instance without paying for a fresh
      lookup each time. For each one give the file:line, the name of the routine it lives in,
      and one phrase on what it contributes. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework would
      have to preserve for that judgement to keep meaning what it means today. A place named
      without the routine it lives in does not count, and neither does a routine named without
      the file:line where it is defined.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: The reachability judgement traced through the routines that make it up, each located
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. Widen back out from that one judgement to the class as a whole,
      as a single subject, and find EVERY place in the codebase that would have to be reviewed
      or changed with it, so the rework cannot silently break one. Most of what you are looking
      for never asks for one of these machines at all. It is already holding one when its own
      work begins: it was handed one when it was constructed and kept it, or it walked to one
      through two or three steps of associated records belonging to something else entirely, or
      it named that walk as text rather than as code, or it got one back from a lookup helper on
      a third thing that quietly reuses an instance it already had. The routine it does its own
      job from is named after that job and never after this class. So the answer you owe has two
      halves for every dependent: HOW that place comes to be holding one - which of those routes
      it is on - and WHOSE behaviour changes, meaning the routine the dependency is inside, its
      file:line, and one phrase on what that routine would start getting wrong if the class
      stopped handing back what it hands back today. Organise the answer by area, one section
      per area, and treat an area as finished only when every dependent inside it is listed
      separately with its own location - if you cannot finish an area, say which one and say
      what you left in it, because an area you quietly summarised reads as an area with nothing
      in it. A file:line with no routine named around it does not count, a routine you cannot
      state the consequence for does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every dependent located at file:line with its route and its own consequence named
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, the record it keeps its state in, the job that refreshes
  # ---- that state and the action that establishes it. Step 1's answer. Both arms reach these
  # ---- and they do NOT score. Every line below was opened and read in this session. None of
  # ---- these four files appears in the `dependents` group.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [app/Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:1287 `$isFunctional = data_get($this->settings, 'is_reachable') && data_get($this->settings, 'is_usable') && data_get($this->settings, 'force_disabled') === false && ! $this->hasPlaceholderIp();` inside `Server::isFunctional()` (def at :1285) - the judgement the whole product gates on, composed out of the settings record and dropping the ssh multiplex socket at :1290 when it comes back false; :537 `return $this->hasOne(ServerSetting::class);` inside `Server::settings()` is where that state is attached, and :230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` with its `static::$identityMapCache` at :240-241 is the per-request identity map every destination accessor bottoms out in"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [app/Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:63-64 `'is_reachable',` / `'is_usable',` in the fillable list (cast to boolean at :110-111) - the two stored flags `isFunctional()` reads; :247 `public function server()` returning `$this->belongsTo(Server::class)` at :249 is the way back to the machine the settings belong to"
  - id: c:validate-server
    group: contract
    match: [app/Actions/Server/ValidateServer.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Server/ValidateServer.php:26 `public function handle(Server $server)` - the action that establishes the state in the first place: it clears `validation_logs` at :28-30, refreshes cloud-provider state at :32 and :45 before anything else, and writes the outcome back onto the machine"
  - id: c:server-check-job
    group: contract
    match: [app/Jobs/ServerCheckJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/ServerCheckJob.php:55 `if ($this->server->serverStatus() === false) {` inside `ServerCheckJob::handle()` (def at :52) - the scheduled work that keeps the judgement current, bailing out at :56 when the machine is not ready and otherwise pulling containers at :60 and fanning out to sentinel and log-drain checks at :67 and :70"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: eighteen rows, ONE PER FILE, across eight areas (app/Models, app/Jobs,
  # ---- app/Listeners, app/Notifications, app/Livewire and three distinct sub-trees of it,
  # ---- bootstrap/helpers, app/Helpers, app/Actions). Every row appears in the shown
  # ---- sense_blast payload quoted in the header. Every line below was opened and read in this
  # ---- session; no credit is taken from a script tally.
  # ----
  # ---- No row was cut as free or unreachable, and the reason is that there is nothing to cut
  # ---- from: `inverse_frequency.py` needs a results root and this repo has no run on disk in
  # ---- any arm (verticals/php-laravel/results holds only the empty dryrun/ and loop/ trees),
  # ---- so no row here has ever been offered to a baseline. That is stated rather than
  # ---- estimated: the first citation ranking for this gold set comes out of the mini-bench.

  # -- route: a caching accessor on a third model. Neither of these two files' dependents ever
  # -- resolves a machine; the destination hands one back out of the identity map.
  - id: d:standalone-docker
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/StandaloneDocker.php]
    relation: "app/Models/StandaloneDocker.php:135 `$server = Server::findCached($this->server_id);` inside `StandaloneDocker::getServerAttribute()` (def at :127) - the docker-network destination intercepts lazy loading and serves the machine out of the per-request identity map, caching it back onto the relation at :139, so every `$resource->destination->server` in the product is really this accessor; it would start handing back a stale instance, or a second copy of one already mutated elsewhere, if the caching contract changed"
  - id: d:swarm-docker
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/SwarmDocker.php]
    relation: "app/Models/SwarmDocker.php:96 `$server = Server::findCached($this->server_id);` inside `SwarmDocker::getServerAttribute()` (def at :88) - the swarm destination's copy of the same interception, with the same relation write-back at :100; it is a separate file with a separate class and a rework that fixes the caching contract in one and not the other splits swarm behaviour from standalone behaviour silently"

  # -- route: handed one at construction and kept. Promoted constructor properties on queued
  # -- work, serialised with the job.
  - id: d:cleanup-helper-containers
    group: dependents
    match: [Jobs/CleanupHelperContainersJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/CleanupHelperContainersJob.php:20 `public function __construct(public Server $server) {}` - the job is constructed around a machine it never looks up and holds it for its whole run: it queries the deployment queue by `server_id` at :26, lists helper containers over it at :39 and removes each one at :70; it would start reaping containers on the wrong machine, or none at all, if what it was handed stopped being the same instance the dispatcher meant"
  - id: d:volume-clone
    group: dependents
    match: [Jobs/VolumeCloneJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/VolumeCloneJob.php:23 `protected Server $sourceServer,` with the nullable `protected ?Server $targetServer,` beside it at :24 - the only dependent that holds TWO machines at once and compares them by identity at :33 `if (! $this->targetServer || $this->targetServer->id === $this->sourceServer->id)` to decide whether the clone is local or crosses machines, then runs commands against each in turn at :52, :74 and :86; it would start cloning a volume onto the source machine, or streaming a copy that did not need to cross the network, if that identity comparison stopped meaning what it means"
  - id: d:push-server-update
    group: dependents
    match: [Jobs/PushServerUpdateJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/PushServerUpdateJob.php:116 `public function __construct(public Server $server, public $data)` - the sentinel push handler holds the machine alongside the raw payload it was pushed, re-hydrates the relations it needs at :163 `$this->server->loadMissing(['settings', 'team']);`, reads its disk-usage threshold out of the settings relation at :179 via a string path, and keys its overlap lock on the machine's uuid at :108; it would start evaluating the wrong machine's thresholds, or deadlocking two pushes together, if the held instance or that settings path moved"
  - id: d:database-backup
    group: dependents
    match: [Jobs/DatabaseBackupJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/DatabaseBackupJob.php:110 `$this->server = $this->database->destination->server;` inside the job's setup, with the service-hosted variant one branch above at :106 `$this->server = $this->database->service->server;` - the backup job walks from the database it was given, through its destination or its service, to the machine, into its own `public Server $server` property declared at :42, and then runs every dump command over it (`instant_remote_process($commands, $this->server, ...)` at :142, :173, :196 and :238); it would start dumping from the wrong machine, or throwing its own 'Server not found?!' at :114, if either walk stopped resolving"
  - id: d:scheduled-task
    group: dependents
    match: [Jobs/ScheduledTaskJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/ScheduledTaskJob.php:91 `$this->server = $this->resource->destination->server;` - the same walk from a polymorphic resource, and this file also reaches the settings record through the walk twice more for the timezone it schedules in (:97 `$this->resource->destination->server->settings->server_timezone` and the service variant at :99); it would start running scheduled commands in the wrong timezone, or against a machine that is no longer the resource's, if the destination link changed shape"

  # -- route: resolved from a raw identifier that arrived from somewhere else entirely.
  - id: d:cloudflare-tunnel-listener
    group: dependents
    match: [Listeners/CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php]
    relation: "app/Listeners/CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php:21 `$this->server = Server::where('id', $server_id)->firstOrFail();` inside `CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification::handle()` (def at :16) - the listener is handed a broadcast event, digs a bare id out of its payload at :18 `data_get($event, 'data.server_id')`, resolves it into the `public Server $server` it declares at :12, then health-checks the tunnel container over it at :29 and REWRITES the machine's address at :59 `$this->server->update(['ip' => $ssh_domain]);` after updating its settings at :52; it would start pointing a machine at the wrong address, or failing hard on an event for a machine since deleted, if that resolution changed"
  - id: d:settings-backup
    group: dependents
    match: [Livewire/SettingsBackup.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/SettingsBackup.php:55 `$this->server = Server::findOrFail(0);` inside `SettingsBackup::mount()` (def at :49) - the instance's own host, addressed by the hardcoded id 0 rather than by any relation, held in the `public Server $server` declared at :22, gated on `$this->server->isFunctional()` at :70 before a backup is allowed and inspected again at :84-85 for the coolify database container; it would start backing up nothing, or backing up against a machine that is not the host, if id 0 stopped being the self-managed row"

  # -- route: walked across relations belonging to some other record, or named as text.
  - id: d:service-index
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Service/Index.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Service/Index.php:149 `$this->server = $this->serviceDatabase->service->destination->server;` inside `Index::initializeDatabaseProperties()` (def at :147) - a THREE-hop walk from the service database, through its service, through its destination, into the `public ?Server $server` declared at :39; the same page repeats the walk inline twice more to gate log streaming (:235 and :393, both `->service->destination->server->isLogDrainEnabled()`); it would start showing a service's logs against the wrong machine, or refusing log drain that is actually enabled, if any hop in that chain changed"
  - id: d:postgres-general
    group: dependents
    match: [Database/Postgresql/General.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Database/Postgresql/General.php:136 `$this->server = data_get($this->database, 'destination.server');` inside `General::mount()` (def at :131) - the walk written as a STRING path rather than as code, so no symbol search resolves it; the result is held in the `public ?Server $server` at :20, checked for null at :137, gated for log drain at :198 and used to run deletion and init-script commands over the machine at :276 and :325; it would start silently leaving the page with no machine at all - the string path returns null rather than raising - if the destination relation were renamed"
  - id: d:clone-me
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/CloneMe.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/CloneMe.php:90 `$this->server = $this->servers->where('id', $server_id)->first();` inside `CloneMe::selectServer()` (def at :79) - the machine is picked out of a collection the component already loaded from the current team at :62-65, which rejects build machines at :65, rather than being fetched; it is held in the `public ?Server $server` at :39; it would start cloning a project onto a machine the team may not use, or onto none, if the team's collection or the build-machine exclusion changed"

  # -- route: taken as a parameter by free functions and static helpers - the choke points every
  # -- remote command in the product flows through.
  - id: d:remote-process
    group: dependents
    match: [helpers/remoteProcess.php]
    relation: "bootstrap/helpers/remoteProcess.php:139 `function instant_remote_process(Collection|array $command, Server $server, bool $throwError = true, ...)` - the free function that every synchronous remote command in this codebase bottoms out in; it asks the machine whether it is running as a non-root user at :143 `if ($server->isNonRoot() && ! $no_sudo)` and rewrites the command with sudo at :144 before handing it to the retry handler at :149; it would start running every command with the wrong privileges, on every machine at once, if that one question changed its answer"
  - id: d:docker-helper
    group: dependents
    match: [helpers/docker.php]
    relation: "bootstrap/helpers/docker.php:1255 `$server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($server_id);` inside `validateComposeFile(string $compose, int $server_id)` (def at :1252) - a free helper that takes an integer id, not an instance, and resolves it through the team scope, throwing at :1258 when it comes back empty before it will parse the compose file at :1260; it would start validating a compose file against a machine belonging to another team, or refusing a valid one, if that ownership scope changed"
  - id: d:ssh-multiplexing
    group: dependents
    match: [Helpers/SshMultiplexingHelper.php]
    relation: "app/Helpers/SshMultiplexingHelper.php:101 `public static function generateScpCommand(Server $server, string $source, string $dest): string` - the static helper that builds file-transfer commands takes the machine as a parameter and interrogates it for the shape of the command, reading its ssh configuration at :103 and branching on `$server->isIpv6()` at :107; it would start emitting a command that cannot connect - wrong key path, wrong address family - for every transfer in the product, and this file also tears down the multiplex socket at :97-98"
  - id: d:stop-application-one-server
    group: dependents
    match: [Application/StopApplicationOneServer.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Application/StopApplicationOneServer.php:13 `public function handle(Application $application, Server $server)` - the action is handed BOTH the resource and the machine and it uses them differently: it walks the application's own destination to test for swarm at :15, and gates on the machine it was passed at :18 `if (! $server->isFunctional())`, returning the string 'Server is not functional' rather than raising, before listing containers over it at :22; it would start stopping containers on an unusable machine, or returning a soft refusal a caller cannot distinguish from success, if that judgement moved"
  - id: d:get-containers-status
    group: dependents
    match: [Docker/GetContainersStatus.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Docker/GetContainersStatus.php:46 `$this->server = $server;` inside `GetContainersStatus::handle()` (def at :42) - the action stashes the machine it was passed into an UNTYPED `public $server` property declared at :34, gates the whole run on it at :47 `if (! $this->server->isFunctional())` and then enumerates the machine's applications at :50; it would start reporting every container as missing across the entire dashboard if that gate flipped, and the untyped property means nothing here would fail loudly first"

  # -- route: carried into a notification's channels.
  - id: d:container-stopped
    group: dependents
    match: [Container/ContainerStopped.php]
    relation: "app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:14 `public function __construct(public string $name, public Server $server, public ?string $url = null)` - the notification holds the machine as a promoted property and reads its name into every channel it renders (mail at :27 and :30, discord at :41, telegram at :54, pushover at :85, slack at :93, and the webhook payload at :113-114 which sends both `server_name` and `server_uuid`); it would start sending alerts that name the wrong machine, on six channels at once, if those attributes changed - and the webhook one is a public contract other systems parse"
